---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PhenotypeLibrary from "../../components/PhenotypeLibrary.astro";
import SitesPicker from "../../components/SitesPicker.astro";
import DesignDiagram from "../../components/DesignDiagram.astro";
import ProtocolCommon from "../../components/ProtocolCommon.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");

// Curated lists surfaced as checkboxes (matching the ACNU/SCCS/CCO/DA builders).
const SUBGROUPS = [
  "Age group (e.g. ≥65 vs <65)",
  "Sex (male vs female)",
  "Region / hospital level",
  "Urban vs rural",
  "Baseline outcome level (high vs low)",
  "Outcome subtype",
  "Provider / prescriber type",
  "Calendar sub-period",
];
const SENSITIVITY = [
  "Alternative interruption date (announcement vs implementation lag)",
  "Exclude a transition / phase-in period around the interruption",
  "Alternative model (Poisson vs negative-binomial vs linear)",
  "Add / remove seasonality terms",
  "Alternative autocorrelation handling (Newey–West / Prais–Winsten / ARIMA)",
  "Controlled vs uncontrolled series",
  "Negative-control outcome or a placebo (pseudo) interruption date",
  "Restrict to the primary outcome definition / setting",
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Interrupted Time Series (ITS) protocol — Daniel Tsai"
  description="A free, in-browser protocol builder for interrupted time series (segmented regression) evaluations of a policy or intervention, with one-click Word / Markdown export."
>
  <header class="relative bg-gradient-to-br from-brand-50 via-white to-brand-100/40 border-b border-slate-200 overflow-hidden">
    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
      <p class="uppercase tracking-widest text-xs text-brand-600 font-semibold mb-2">Protocol Generator · ITS</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-slate-900">Interrupted Time Series protocol</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base text-slate-600 max-w-3xl">
        <strong>In plain terms.</strong> Something changed on a known date. Take the outcome as it was trending
        <em>before</em> that date, draw that trend forward as "what would have happened anyway", and ask how far the
        outcome afterwards departs from it — both as an immediate jump and as a change of direction.
      </p>
      <details class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-brand-700">The two things a segmented regression separates, and why it matters</summary>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The outcome is aggregated into equal time intervals and regressed on three terms: <strong>time</strong>
          (the pre-intervention slope), an <strong>interruption indicator</strong> (1 from the first affected period
          onward), and <strong>time-since-interruption</strong> (0 until the interruption, then counting up). Their
          coefficients are the baseline trend, the <strong>change in level</strong> — the immediate step at the
          interruption — and the <strong>change in slope</strong> — how the trend itself bends afterwards. They answer
          different questions and can point in opposite directions: a policy can cause a sharp one-off drop that then
          drifts back up (level change down, slope change up), and reporting only one of the two would describe it
          wrongly (Wagner 2002; Lopez Bernal 2017).
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <strong>Two things routinely break it.</strong> <em>Seasonality</em>: if the outcome has an annual cycle and
          the interruption falls at a particular point in that cycle, the cycle is read as a step or a bend.
          <em>Autocorrelation</em>: adjacent time points resemble each other, which does not bias the coefficients but
          makes the standard errors too small and the confidence intervals too narrow. Neither is optional to consider.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <strong>Why a control series strengthens the claim.</strong> An uncontrolled ITS attributes <em>every</em>
          departure from the extrapolated pre-trend to the intervention — including anything else that happened at the
          same time, and any secular change that was already bending. A comparison series that shares the secular
          environment but is not exposed to the intervention lets you difference those out: the estimate becomes the
          <em>difference</em> in level and slope changes, and a co-intervention affecting both series cancels
          (Lopez Bernal 2018).
        </p>
      </details>
      <a class="mt-4 inline-block text-sm text-brand-700 hover:underline font-semibold" href={`${base}/tools/protocol-generator`}>← All designs</a>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
    <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1fr] items-start">
      <!-- Form -->
      <form id="form" class="protocol-form space-y-6">
        <fieldset class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 space-y-4">
          <legend class="px-2 text-sm font-bold text-brand-700">1 · Identity</legend>
          <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Study title</span>
            <input name="title" placeholder="e.g. Effect of the 2019 reimbursement policy on monthly SGLT2-inhibitor initiation" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm" /></label>
          <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Data source(s)</span>
            <input name="data" placeholder="e.g. Taiwan NHIRD (2015–2022)" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm" /></label>
          <SitesPicker target="data" />
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 space-y-4">
          <legend class="px-2 text-sm font-bold text-brand-700">2 · Question</legend>
          <div>
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
              <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Population</span>
              <button type="button" data-open-library data-category="indications" data-target-name="population" class="text-[11px] text-brand-700 font-semibold hover:underline">📚 Library</button>
            </div>
            <input name="population" placeholder="e.g. Adults with type 2 diabetes in Taiwan" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
              <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Outcome (a rate, count, or level over time)</span>
              <button type="button" data-open-library data-category="outcomes" data-target-name="outcome" class="text-[11px] text-brand-700 font-semibold hover:underline">📚 Library</button>
            </div>
            <input name="outcome" placeholder="e.g. Monthly rate of new SGLT2-inhibitor initiations per 1,000 patients" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm" />
          </div>
          <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Intervention / policy (the interruption)</span>
            <input name="intervention" placeholder="e.g. National Health Insurance reimbursement expansion for SGLT2 inhibitors" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm" /></label>
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 space-y-4">
          <legend class="px-2 text-sm font-bold text-brand-700">3 · Time series &amp; interruption</legend>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3">
            <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Interruption date</span>
              <input name="interruptDate" placeholder="e.g. 2019-04" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm" /></label>
            <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Time unit</span>
              <select name="unit" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm">
                <option>monthly</option><option>quarterly</option><option>every 6 months</option><option>yearly</option><option>weekly</option>
              </select></label>
            <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Transition / phase-in</span>
              <input name="lag" placeholder="e.g. exclude 2 months around the date" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm" /></label>
          </div>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3">
            <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Pre-intervention points</span>
              <input type="number" name="prePoints" value="24" min="3" step="1" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm" /></label>
            <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Post-intervention points</span>
              <input type="number" name="postPoints" value="24" min="3" step="1" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm" /></label>
          </div>
          <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Denominator / offset (for rates)</span>
            <input name="denom" placeholder="e.g. person-months of the eligible population (log offset)" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm" /></label>
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 space-y-4">
          <legend class="px-2 text-sm font-bold text-brand-700">4 · Model specification</legend>
          <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Regression model</span>
            <select name="model" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm">
              <option value="poisson">Segmented Poisson / negative-binomial (rates or counts)</option>
              <option value="linear">Segmented linear regression (OLS; continuous outcome)</option>
            </select></label>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3">
            <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Seasonality</span>
              <select name="seasonality" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm">
                <option value="none">None</option>
                <option value="harmonic">Harmonic (Fourier) terms</option>
                <option value="month">Calendar-month / quarter dummies</option>
              </select></label>
            <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Autocorrelation</span>
              <select name="autocorr" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm">
                <option value="none">None — check Durbin–Watson / ACF</option>
                <option value="newey">Newey–West (HAC) robust SE</option>
                <option value="prais">Prais–Winsten / Cochrane–Orcutt</option>
                <option value="arima">ARIMA errors</option>
              </select></label>
          </div>
          <label class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-slate-700">
            <input type="checkbox" name="controlled" class="mt-0.5 rounded border-slate-400 text-brand-700 focus:ring-brand-500" />
            <span><strong>Controlled ITS</strong> — add a comparison series unaffected by the intervention (a control group / region / outcome) to strengthen causal inference.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="block"><span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Control series (if controlled)</span>
            <input name="controlSeries" placeholder="e.g. Same outcome in a region where the policy did not apply" class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-sm" /></label>
          <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-500 border-t border-slate-100 pt-3">
            <strong>If you intend to run this in RWE Studio.</strong> Every option here is a legitimate protocol
            choice — you may well execute the analysis in R, SAS or Stata. But the Studio's in-browser ITS does not
            implement all of them, so pick deliberately rather than being surprised later. It runs: the segmented
            Poisson / negative-binomial and linear models, calendar-period and harmonic seasonality, a control series
            with group-by-segment interactions, and Newey–West (HAC) standard errors.
            It does <strong>not</strong> implement <strong>Prais–Winsten or ARIMA errors</strong>; it has no way to
            <strong>exclude a transition / phase-in period</strong> around the interruption; and it reports the
            difference from the counterfactual <strong>at the end of follow-up</strong> rather than at a time point
            you nominate.
          </p>
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 space-y-4">
          <legend class="px-2 text-sm font-bold text-brand-700">5 · Subgroups &amp; sensitivity</legend>
          <div>
            <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Subgroup analyses (tick to perform)</span>
            <div class="mt-1.5 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
              {SUBGROUPS.map((sg, i) => (
                <label class="flex items-start gap-2 text-xs text-slate-700">
                  <input type="checkbox" name="subgroupsPick" value={sg} checked={i < 2} class="mt-0.5 rounded border-slate-400 text-brand-700 focus:ring-brand-500" />
                  <span>{sg}</span>
                </label>
              ))}
            </div>
            <textarea name="subgroupsCustom" rows="2" class="mt-2 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-xs" placeholder="Custom subgroups (one per line)"></textarea>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-700 font-semibold">Sensitivity analyses (tick to perform)</span>
            <div class="mt-1.5 grid grid-cols-1 gap-y-1">
              {SENSITIVITY.map((sn, i) => (
                <label class="flex items-start gap-2 text-xs text-slate-700">
                  <input type="checkbox" name="sensitivityPick" value={sn} checked={i < 5} class="mt-0.5 rounded border-slate-400 text-brand-700 focus:ring-brand-500" />
                  <span>{sn}</span>
                </label>
              ))}
            </div>
            <textarea name="sensitivityCustom" rows="2" class="mt-2 w-full rounded-lg border border-slate-300 p-2 text-xs" placeholder="Custom sensitivity analyses (one per line)"></textarea>
          </div>
        </fieldset>

        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <button type="button" data-action="download-docx" class="bg-brand-700 text-white hover:bg-brand-800 font-semibold px-5 py-2.5 rounded-lg text-sm">⬇ Word (.docx)</button>
          <button type="button" data-action="download-md" class="bg-white border border-brand-700 text-brand-700 hover:bg-brand-50 font-semibold px-5 py-2.5 rounded-lg text-sm">⬇ Markdown</button>
          <button type="button" data-action="copy" class="text-brand-700 hover:underline font-semibold text-sm">Copy</button>
          <button type="button" data-action="reset" class="ml-auto text-slate-500 hover:text-rose-600 hover:underline text-sm font-semibold">Clear all</button>
          <span class="basis-full text-[11px] text-slate-400">Auto-saved to this browser.</span>
          <p data-action-msg class="basis-full text-xs"></p>
        </div>
      </form>

      <!-- Preview -->
      <div class="lg:sticky lg:top-20 self-start">
        <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-slate-500 font-semibold mb-2">Live protocol preview</p>
        <pre id="preview" class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-5 text-xs leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap overflow-auto max-h-[70vh]"></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-sm">
      <h2 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Consistency checks <span class="text-xs font-normal text-slate-400">· ITS 防呆</span></h2>
      <div id="checks" class="mt-3 space-y-1.5"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">Study design diagram</h2>
      <p id="dcap" class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-500"></p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><div id="diagram"></div></div>
      <div id="dlegend" class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1.5 text-xs text-slate-600"></div>
    </div>

    <PhenotypeLibrary />
    <DesignDiagram />
    <ProtocolCommon />
  </section>

  <script src="https://unpkg.com/docx@8.5.0/build/index.umd.js" is:inline></script>
  <script is:inline>
    const STORAGE_KEY = "daniel.its-builder";
    const form = document.getElementById("form");
    const preview = document.getElementById("preview");
    const V = (n) => (form.elements[n]?.value || "").trim();
    const CH = (n) => !!(form.elements[n] && form.elements[n].checked);
    const MV = (n) => [...form.querySelectorAll('[name="' + n + '"]:checked')].map((c) => c.value);
    const mergeList = (pick, custom) => [...(pick || []), ...String(custom || "").split(/\r?\n/).map((x) => x.trim()).filter(Boolean)];
    const or = (s, d) => (s && s.length ? s : d);
    const esc = (x) => String(x == null ? "" : x).replace(/[&<>"']/g, (c) => ({ "&": "&amp;", "<": "&lt;", ">": "&gt;", '"': "&quot;", "'": "&#39;" }[c]));

    // A saved draft or a ?seed= link can carry a value one of these <select>s
    // does not offer — a renamed option, a link from an older version of this
    // page, someone else's builder. `el.value = v` then leaves selectedIndex at
    // -1, the select shows blank, and a select with no selection contributes
    // NOTHING to the form, so readForm's `|| "monthly"` / `|| "poisson"` fill in
    // a choice the draft never made and the protocol asserts it as the user's.
    // Four selects here, so a stale draft could silently rewrite the time unit,
    // the model, the seasonality and the inference all at once, with a checks
    // panel that then reported the substitutions as facts ("No seasonality
    // adjustment with monthly data" for a draft that said neither).
    //
    // The record has to travel IN the saved draft under a reserved key: render()
    // runs immediately after restore and writes the substituted state back over
    // the draft, so a variable-only record survives exactly one page load and
    // the user's original values are gone with it. And it must be dismissible,
    // because re-picking the option a select is already showing fires no
    // `change` event, leaving a blocker the user has no way to answer.
    const STALE_KEY = "__staleSelects";
    const SELECTS = [...form.querySelectorAll("select")].map((el) => ({
      name: el.name,
      label: (el.closest("label") ? (el.closest("label").querySelector("span") || {}).textContent : "") || el.name,
      options: [...el.options].map((o) => o.value),
      fallback: (([...el.options].find((o) => o.defaultSelected) || el.options[0] || {}).value) || "",
    }));
    let staleSelects = [];
    const noteStale = (obj) => {
      if (!obj || typeof obj !== "object") return;
      SELECTS.forEach((sel) => {
        const v = obj[sel.name];
        // The empty string was exempted here, and it is the one value that
        // most needs catching: a draft or ?seed= link carrying "" blanks the
        // select exactly as an unoffered value does, and readForm's `|| "monthly"`
        // then substitutes just as silently — but with nothing recorded, so no
        // blocker fired at all. An empty string is not a value this page offers,
        // which is the only test that matters here.
        if (v == null) return;
        if (sel.options.indexOf(String(v)) < 0 && !staleSelects.some((x) => x.name === sel.name)) {
          staleSelects.push({ name: sel.name, label: String(sel.label).trim(), saved: String(v), used: sel.fallback });
        }
      });
    };
    // Two renderings, because this goes to two audiences. On screen it must say
    // what to press; in the exported protocol there is no button to press, and
    // an instruction to press one reads as a defect in the document.
    const staleMsg = (x) => x.saved === ""
      ? "“" + x.label + "” was restored with no value at all — the draft or link it came from left it blank. The field has been set to “" + x.used + "” and this protocol describes THAT, which is a choice nobody made."
      : "“" + x.label + "” was saved as “" + x.saved + "”, and this page does not offer that choice. The field has been set to “" + x.used + "” and this protocol describes THAT — a different specification from the one the saved draft named.";
    const staleMsgs = () => staleSelects.map((x) => staleMsg(x) + " Pick the option you want, or press “Keep “" + x.used + "”” to confirm it.");
    const staleMsgsDoc = () => staleSelects.map(staleMsg);

    const readForm = () => ({
      title: V("title"), data: V("data"), population: V("population"), outcome: V("outcome"), intervention: V("intervention"),
      interruptDate: V("interruptDate"), unit: V("unit") || "monthly", lag: V("lag"),
      // Raw, deliberately. These used to arrive here as `V("prePoints") || "24"`,
      // so an emptied box exported a protocol asserting 24 points the user never
      // chose — and the "only N points" warning went quiet at the same moment,
      // because 24 clears the ≥ 8 bar. What the box holds is read once, below.
      prePoints: V("prePoints"), postPoints: V("postPoints"), denom: V("denom"),
      model: V("model") || "poisson", seasonality: V("seasonality") || "none", autocorr: V("autocorr") || "none",
      controlled: CH("controlled"), controlSeries: V("controlSeries"),
      subgroupsPick: MV("subgroupsPick"), subgroupsCustom: V("subgroupsCustom"), sensitivityPick: MV("sensitivityPick"), sensitivityCustom: V("sensitivityCustom"),
    });

    // ONE reading of a period count, shared by the protocol text, the diagram,
    // the consistency checks and both exports, so they cannot tell three
    // different stories about the same box. `<input type="number">` accepts
    // "1e3" and "24.5" as valid — nothing on screen objects to either — and
    // `parseInt` then reads them as 1 and 24. A count nobody can read is not a
    // count of 24 and not a count of 1; it is unreadable, and this says so.
    // "1e3" IS a number and reads as 1000, matching how the other builders read
    // their day counts; a fractional number of periods is refused, because half
    // an interval is not a thing the design can have.
    const readPts = (raw) => {
      const t = String(raw == null ? "" : raw).trim();
      if (!t) return { raw: t, n: null, state: "blank" };
      // Decimal only. Number() also parses "0x10" as 16 and "0b11" as 3, and
      // nobody typing those into a box labelled "Pre-intervention points" means
      // sixteen or three — that is a guess dressed as a reading. A thousands
      // separator ("1,000") is refused for the same reason: Number() would read
      // it as NaN here but the same trap has produced a tenfold-wrong answer
      // elsewhere in this repo, so it is refused by name rather than by luck.
      if (!/^[+-]?(\d+(\.\d*)?|\.\d+)(e[+-]?\d+)?$/i.test(t)) return { raw: t, n: null, state: "bad" };
      const v = Number(t);
      if (!isFinite(v) || !Number.isInteger(v) || v < 0) return { raw: t, n: null, state: "bad" };
      return { raw: t, n: v, state: "ok" };
    };
    const ptsText = (p) => p.state === "ok" ? String(p.n)
      : p.state === "blank" ? "(not specified)"
      : "(not specified — “" + p.raw + "” could not be read as a whole number of periods)";
    const prePts = (s) => readPts(s.prePoints);
    const postPts = (s) => readPts(s.postPoints);

    // The design matrix's coding, stated in the protocol rather than left on
    // screen. §6 calls the level-change coefficient "the immediate step at the
    // interruption", which is true ONLY under this coding: with the
    // post-intervention counter starting at 1 instead of 0, the step at the
    // first affected period is the level coefficient PLUS the slope
    // coefficient. That is the error the corrigendum to the tutorial this
    // protocol cites exists because of, and it is common enough to have its own
    // methods paper. A protocol that names "change in level" as an estimand
    // without saying how time-since-interruption is coded is not reproducible,
    // and this sentence used to live only in the consistency panel — the one
    // surface that never reached the exported document.
    const CODING_NOTE = "Under this coding the change-in-level coefficient is the immediate step at the interruption itself and the change in slope accrues from the following period; under the alternative coding, in which the counter starts at 1, the same coefficient is not the immediate step, so the two parameterisations are not interchangeable and the coefficients from one must not be read as the other's (Lopez Bernal 2017 corrigendum; Xiao 2021).";
    // "every 6 months" is written as a phrase, not an adjective, so every
    // sentence that used the raw value read "aggregated into equal every 6
    // months intervals". One map, used everywhere a unit modifies a noun.
    const unitPhrase = (u) => (u === "every 6 months" ? "six-monthly" : (u || "monthly"));
    const modelText = (m) => m === "linear"
      ? "segmented linear (ordinary least squares) regression on the continuous outcome"
      : "segmented Poisson regression (with a negative-binomial alternative if overdispersed) on the aggregated rate/count, using a log link and a log-population / person-time offset";
    const seasonText = (s) => ({
      none: "No explicit seasonality adjustment is applied (justify only if the outcome is non-seasonal)",
      harmonic: "Seasonality is modelled with harmonic (Fourier sine/cosine) terms",
      month: "Seasonality is modelled with calendar-month (or quarter) indicator terms",
    })[s] || "No explicit seasonality adjustment";
    const autocorrText = (a) => ({
      none: "Residual autocorrelation is assessed with ACF/PACF plots and the Durbin–Watson statistic (which is read descriptively rather than as a formal test when the model is a Poisson or negative-binomial fit, where it has no standard null distribution)",
      newey: "Newey–West heteroskedasticity- and autocorrelation-consistent (HAC) standard errors are used, with the lag/bandwidth rule and its maximum lag pre-specified rather than chosen after inspecting the residuals",
      // Prais–Winsten and Cochrane–Orcutt are not the same estimator: the first
      // retains the initial observation through the sqrt(1 - rho^2) transform
      // and the second drops it. On a series this short that is one of the few
      // pre-intervention points the whole counterfactual rests on, so the
      // protocol has to say which.
      prais: "A Prais–Winsten correction for first-order autocorrelation is applied, retaining the first observation through the sqrt(1 - rho-squared) transform; if the Cochrane–Orcutt variant is used instead, which drops that observation, this is stated together with how many pre-intervention points remain",
      arima: "Autocorrelated errors are modelled with an ARIMA structure (order chosen by AIC and residual diagnostics)",
    })[a] || "Residual autocorrelation is assessed with the Durbin–Watson statistic";
    // Reads `controlled`, which it did not. §6 already said the difference in
    // level and slope changes "is the primary estimate" as soon as the box was
    // ticked, while §7 — the section headed "Estimand" — went on naming the
    // undifferenced within-series changes. Two adjacent sections of one
    // protocol declaring two different primary estimands, and on the log scale
    // the difference is not a rate ratio at all: the group-by-segment
    // interaction is a RATIO of the two series' rate ratios.
    const effectText = (s) => s.controlled
      ? (s.model === "linear"
        ? "the DIFFERENCE between the intervention and control series in the change in level and in the change in slope (a difference-in-differences on the outcome's natural scale), with 95% confidence intervals"
        : "the RATIO of the intervention series' level change to the control series' level change, and the same for the slope change — the group-by-segment interaction terms, which on the log scale are ratios of rate ratios rather than rate ratios — with 95% confidence intervals")
      : (s.model === "linear"
        ? "the change in level (immediate step) and the change in slope (trend), both on the outcome's natural scale, with 95% confidence intervals"
        : "the change in level and the change in slope expressed as rate ratios (incidence-rate ratios) with 95% confidence intervals");
    // The counterfactual is not the same object in the two designs. In a
    // controlled ITS the intervention series' own extrapolated pre-trend is
    // precisely what the control series exists to replace, so naming it under a
    // controlled design describes the analysis the control was added to avoid.
    const cfText = (s) => s.controlled
      ? "the counterfactual (the intervention series' pre-intervention trend carried forward with the change the control series shows over the same periods — not its own extrapolated pre-trend, which is the object the control series exists to replace)"
      : "the counterfactual (this series' extrapolated pre-intervention trend)";
    // The contrast with the counterfactual grows with how far past the
    // interruption you look — it is linear in the slope-change coefficient — so
    // "the difference from the counterfactual" is a family of estimands until a
    // horizon is fixed. The protocol used to call that horizon "pre-specified"
    // while the form collected nothing that could specify it.
    const HORIZON_AT = "at the end of the post-intervention series";
    const HORIZON_WHY = "That horizon is stated because the difference from the counterfactual grows with how far past the interruption it is measured — it is linear in the change-in-slope coefficient, so it is a family of numbers rather than one. Any additional horizon must be named in this protocol before the analysis is run.";
    // One map per select, used by the design-summary table and by the analysis
    // steps alike. They were separate copies with their own fallbacks.
    // One list, used by both exports. Verified against search records rather
    // than from memory; the corrigendum was cited as 2020 while volume 50
    // issue 3 of that journal is 2021, and the parameterisation question the
    // corrigendum exists because of had no citation at all until §4 started
    // stating the coding.
    const REFS = [
      "Wagner AK, Soumerai SB, Zhang F, Ross-Degnan D. Segmented regression analysis of interrupted time series studies in medication use research. J Clin Pharm Ther 2002;27(4):299-309. doi:10.1046/j.1365-2710.2002.00430.x",
      "Lopez Bernal J, Cummins S, Gasparrini A. Interrupted time series regression for the evaluation of public health interventions: a tutorial. Int J Epidemiol 2017;46(1):348-355. doi:10.1093/ije/dyw098",
      "Lopez Bernal J, Cummins S, Gasparrini A. Corrigendum to: Interrupted time series regression for the evaluation of public health interventions: a tutorial. Int J Epidemiol 2021;50(3):1045. doi:10.1093/ije/dyaa118",
      "Xiao H, Augusto O, Wagenaar BH. Reflection on modern methods: a common error in the segmented regression parameterization of interrupted time-series analyses. Int J Epidemiol 2021;50(3):1011-1015. doi:10.1093/ije/dyaa148",
      "Lopez Bernal J, Cummins S, Gasparrini A. The use of controls in interrupted time series studies of public health interventions. Int J Epidemiol 2018;47(6):2082-2093. doi:10.1093/ije/dyy135",
      "Kontopantelis E, Doran T, Springate DA, Buchan I, Reeves D. Regression based quasi-experimental approach when randomisation is not an option: interrupted time series analysis. BMJ 2015;350:h2750. doi:10.1136/bmj.h2750",
      "Penfold RB, Zhang F. Use of interrupted time series analysis in evaluating health care quality improvements. Acad Pediatr 2013;13(6 Suppl):S38-S44. doi:10.1016/j.acap.2013.08.002",
      "Zhang F, Wagner AK, Ross-Degnan D. Simulation-based power calculation for designing interrupted time series analyses of health policy interventions. J Clin Epidemiol 2011;64(11):1252-1261. PMID 21640554",
      "Liu W, Ye S, Barton BA, et al. Simulation-based power and sample size calculation for designing interrupted time series analyses of count outcomes in evaluation of health policy interventions. Contemp Clin Trials Commun 2020;17:100474. PMID 31886433",
    ];
    const SEASON_LABEL = { none: "None", harmonic: "Harmonic (Fourier) terms", month: "Calendar-month / quarter dummies" };
    const AC_LABEL = { none: "Durbin–Watson / ACF check, model-based standard errors", newey: "Newey–West (HAC) standard errors", prais: "Prais–Winsten", arima: "ARIMA errors" };

    // Normalised inputs for the shared HARPER/TARGET common sections (window.PC).
    const pcOf = (s) => ({
      design: (s.controlled ? "controlled " : "single-group ") + "interrupted time series (segmented regression)",
      tte: false,
      population: or(s.population, "(population)"),
      exposure: or(s.intervention, "(intervention/policy)"),
      comparator: "",
      outcome: or(s.outcome, "(outcome)"),
      data: s.data,
      // The abstract used to describe a single-group analysis whatever the box
      // said: "Design. controlled interrupted time series" two sentences above
      // an Analysis line with no control series in it. The HARPER abstract is
      // the first thing a reviewer reads and often the only thing.
      analysis: "segmented " + (s.model === "linear" ? "linear" : "Poisson") + " regression estimating the change in level and change in slope at the interruption"
        + (s.controlled ? ", differenced against a control series (" + or(s.controlSeries, "not yet named") + ") with group-by-segment interactions, so the primary estimate is the difference in those changes" : "")
        + (s.autocorr === "none" ? "" : ", with autocorrelation-adjusted inference"),
      groupBy: s.controlled ? "series and period (intervention vs control, pre- vs post-intervention)" : "period (pre- vs post-intervention)",
      limitations: "Co-interventions or secular changes coinciding with the interruption can confound an uncontrolled ITS; " + (s.controlled ? "the control series mitigates this." : "a control series would strengthen inference."),
      // ProtocolCommon's default study-size paragraph is written for a
      // person-level cohort: "power ... from observed exposure and outcome
      // frequencies", "available person-time and event counts". None of that
      // exists here. In an uncontrolled ITS there is no exposure frequency at
      // all — after the date, everyone is exposed — and the quantities that do
      // decide the answer are the two the form already collects and three
      // properties of the series. tailSections reads nz(s.studySize, ...) for
      // exactly this, and ACNU already skips the section outright, so supplying
      // a design-appropriate paragraph is the house-sanctioned route rather
      // than a change to the shared component.
      studySize: "An interrupted time series is sized by the series, not by a count of exposed and unexposed people. What decides the answer is: the number of pre- and post-intervention points (here " + ptsText(prePts(s)) + " and " + ptsText(postPts(s)) + "), the residual variance or overdispersion of the aggregated outcome, the residual autocorrelation — which reduces the effective number of independent observations below the nominal count — where in the series the interruption falls, and which target is being powered, since a change in slope generally needs a longer post-intervention series to detect than a one-off change in level does."
        + (s.controlled ? " With a control series, power depends further on how closely the two series track each other before the interruption." : "")
        + " Power is therefore established by simulation from a pre-intervention series of the same length and variability, at the level and slope changes considered clinically important, rather than from an event-count formula (Zhang 2011 for the segmented linear case; Liu 2020 for count outcomes). Feasibility is confirmed before the analysis is executed: that the outcome"
        + (s.model === "poisson" ? " and its denominator" : "") + " can be aggregated into every period of the window, and that no period is missing — a gap closed up rather than left as a gap turns a per-period coefficient into a per-observed-row coefficient.",
      plannedOutputs: ["Observed series with the fitted pre/post segments and the extrapolated counterfactual (the core ITS figure).", "Level-change and slope-change estimates with 95% CIs."],
    });

    // ── Custom ITS diagram: pre/post fitted segments, interruption line, counterfactual ──
    function renderScheme(s) {
      const pp = prePts(s), qp = postPts(s);
      const DRAW_DEFAULT = 24;
      const pre = Math.max(2, Math.min(36, pp.state === "ok" && pp.n > 0 ? pp.n : DRAW_DEFAULT));
      const post = Math.max(2, Math.min(36, qp.state === "ok" && qp.n > 0 ? qp.n : DRAW_DEFAULT));
      const N = pre + post;
      const W = 860, H = 300, plotL = 64, plotR = W - 20, plotT = 34, plotB = H - 46;
      const xF = (u) => plotL + u * (plotR - plotL);
      const yF = (v) => plotB - ((v - 10) / 80) * (plotB - plotT);
      const uAt = (k) => (N === 1 ? 0 : k / (N - 1));
      const uI = (pre - 0.5) / (N - 1);                 // interruption fraction (between pre and post)
      // Illustrative fitted lines in value-space (0..~70)
      const vpre = (u) => 40 + 22 * u;                  // gentle upward baseline trend
      const level = -15;                                // downward level change at the interruption
      const spost = -34;                                // downward slope change after
      const vpost = (u) => vpre(u) + level + spost * (u - uI);
      const jit = (i) => (((i * 9301 + 49297) % 233280) / 233280 - 0.5) * 6;

      let svg = '<svg viewBox="0 0 ' + W + ' ' + H + '" class="w-full h-auto" style="min-width:640px" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">';
      // axes
      svg += '<line x1="' + plotL + '" y1="' + plotT + '" x2="' + plotL + '" y2="' + plotB + '" stroke="#cbd5e1"/>';
      svg += '<line x1="' + plotL + '" y1="' + plotB + '" x2="' + plotR + '" y2="' + plotB + '" stroke="#cbd5e1"/>';
      svg += '<text x="' + plotL + '" y="' + (plotT - 12) + '" font-size="10" fill="#94a3b8">Outcome (' + esc(s.model === "linear" ? "level" : "rate") + ')</text>';
      svg += '<text x="' + plotR + '" y="' + (plotB + 30) + '" text-anchor="end" font-size="10" fill="#94a3b8">Time (' + esc(s.unit || "monthly") + ') →</text>';
      // interruption band / line
      const xi = xF(uI);
      svg += '<line x1="' + xi + '" y1="' + (plotT - 4) + '" x2="' + xi + '" y2="' + (plotB) + '" stroke="#0f766e" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>';
      svg += '<text x="' + xi + '" y="' + (plotT - 14) + '" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" fill="#0f766e">' + esc(or(s.intervention, "Intervention")) + (s.interruptDate ? " · " + esc(s.interruptDate) : "") + '</text>';
      // counterfactual (dashed continuation of the pre-trend)
      svg += '<line x1="' + xi + '" y1="' + yF(vpre(uI)) + '" x2="' + xF(1) + '" y2="' + yF(vpre(1)) + '" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>';
      // fitted pre line
      svg += '<line x1="' + xF(0) + '" y1="' + yF(vpre(0)) + '" x2="' + xi + '" y2="' + yF(vpre(uI)) + '" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="2.5"/>';
      // fitted post line
      svg += '<line x1="' + xi + '" y1="' + yF(vpost(uI)) + '" x2="' + xF(1) + '" y2="' + yF(vpost(1)) + '" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2.5"/>';
      // level-change bracket at the interruption
      const yCf = yF(vpre(uI)), yPost = yF(vpost(uI));
      svg += '<line x1="' + (xi + 8) + '" y1="' + yCf + '" x2="' + (xi + 8) + '" y2="' + yPost + '" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="1"/>';
      svg += '<text x="' + (xi + 13) + '" y="' + ((yCf + yPost) / 2 + 3) + '" font-size="9.5" fill="#334155">level change</text>';
      svg += '<text x="' + xF(0.8) + '" y="' + (yF(vpost(0.8)) - 8) + '" font-size="9.5" fill="#10b981" font-weight="600">slope change</text>';
      // observed points (scatter around the fitted segments)
      for (let k = 0; k < N; k++) {
        const u = uAt(k), isPre = k < pre;
        const v = (isPre ? vpre(u) : vpost(u)) + jit(k + (isPre ? 0 : 100));
        svg += '<circle cx="' + xF(u) + '" cy="' + yF(v) + '" r="2.6" fill="' + (isPre ? "#8b5cf6" : "#10b981") + '" fill-opacity="0.55"/>';
      }
      // Drag handles: interruption → # pre points; right end → # post points.
      svg += '<circle cx="' + xi + '" cy="' + ((yF(vpre(uI)) + yF(vpost(uI))) / 2) + '" r="5" fill="#fff" stroke="#0f766e" stroke-width="2" class="its-handle" data-field="prePoints" style="cursor:ew-resize;touch-action:none"/>';
      svg += '<circle cx="' + xF(1) + '" cy="' + yF(vpost(1)) + '" r="5" fill="#fff" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2" class="its-handle" data-field="postPoints" style="cursor:ew-resize;touch-action:none"/>';
      const diag = document.getElementById("diagram");
      diag.innerHTML = svg + "</svg>";
      document.getElementById("dlegend").innerHTML =
        '<span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5"><span class="inline-block w-3 h-3 rounded-sm" style="background:#8b5cf6"></span>Pre-intervention trend</span>' +
        '<span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5"><span class="inline-block w-3 h-3 rounded-sm" style="background:#10b981"></span>Post-intervention trend</span>' +
        '<span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5"><span class="inline-block w-3 h-0.5" style="border-top:2px dashed #f59e0b"></span>Counterfactual (expected without the intervention)</span>' +
        '<span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5"><span class="inline-block w-3 h-3" style="border-left:2px dashed #0f766e"></span>Interruption</span>';
      // The drawing clamps at 36 points per segment; say so rather than letting the
      // caption quote a number the picture is not showing. This used to be
      // `parseInt(s.prePoints) > 36`, which is 1 > 36 for "1e3" — so at exactly
      // the input the disclosure was written for, the caption confidently
      // quoted 1000 points over a picture of two and said nothing.
      const clamped = (pp.state === "ok" && pp.n > 36) || (qp.state === "ok" && qp.n > 36);
      const unread = pp.state !== "ok" || qp.state !== "ok" || pp.n === 0 || qp.n === 0;
      document.getElementById("dcap").textContent =
        "Segmented regression fits the pre-intervention trend (" + ptsText(pp) + " " + unitPhrase(s.unit) + " point(s)) and projects it forward as the counterfactual; the post-intervention segment (" + ptsText(qp) + " point(s)) is contrasted against it to estimate the immediate level change and the change in slope" + (s.controlled ? ", differenced against the control series" : "") + ". Drag the ○ handles to change the pre / post point counts. Schematic — the shapes are illustrative, not your data" +
        (clamped ? ", and the drawing shows at most 36 points per segment (" + pre + " before, " + post + " after)" : "") +
        (unread ? ". The drawing falls back to an illustrative " + pre + " / " + post + " points because at least one of the two boxes is empty, zero, or holds something that is not a whole number of periods — it is NOT showing your series" : "") + ".";

      // ── Drag-to-edit (frozen px/point so the drag stays linear) ──
      const svgXof = (clientX) => { const el = diag.querySelector("svg"); if (!el || !el.getScreenCTM) return null; const pt = el.createSVGPoint(); pt.x = clientX; pt.y = 0; const ctm = el.getScreenCTM(); if (!ctm) return null; return pt.matrixTransform(ctm.inverse()).x; };
      const pxPerPoint = (plotR - plotL) / Math.max(1, N - 1);
      diag.querySelectorAll(".its-handle").forEach((h) => {
        h.addEventListener("pointerdown", (ev) => {
          ev.preventDefault(); ev.stopPropagation();
          if (window.__diagDrag) window.__diagDrag.cleanup();
          const field = h.getAttribute("data-field");
          const startVal = Math.max(3, parseInt(form.elements[field] && form.elements[field].value) || 24);
          const pid = ev.pointerId, x0 = svgXof(ev.clientX);
          try { diag.setPointerCapture(pid); } catch (e) {}
          let pending = null, raf = 0;
          const apply = () => { raf = 0; if (pending == null) return; const el = form.elements[field]; if (el) { el.value = pending; render(); } };
          const move = (e) => {
            if (e.pointerId !== pid) return;
            const x = svgXof(e.clientX); if (x == null || x0 == null) return;
            // Clamp the drag to what the drawing can actually show (36), so the
            // handle never keeps raising a number the picture has stopped tracking.
            pending = Math.max(3, Math.min(36, startVal + Math.round((x - x0) / pxPerPoint)));
            if (!raf) raf = requestAnimationFrame(apply);
          };
          const end = (e) => {
            if (e && e.pointerId != null && e.pointerId !== pid) return;
            document.removeEventListener("pointermove", move);
            document.removeEventListener("pointerup", end);
            document.removeEventListener("pointercancel", end);
            if (raf) { cancelAnimationFrame(raf); raf = 0; } apply();
            try { diag.releasePointerCapture(pid); } catch (e2) {}
            window.__diagDrag = null;
          };
          window.__diagDrag = { cleanup: end };
          document.addEventListener("pointermove", move);
          document.addEventListener("pointerup", end);
          document.addEventListener("pointercancel", end);
        });
      });
    }

    // What is still blank. A protocol that reads as finished while its defining
    // elements are placeholders is worse than one that says so, so the gap list
    // travels into the preview, the Markdown and the Word file alike.
    const missing = (s) => {
      const pp = prePts(s), qp = postPts(s);
      // A segment count of zero is not a short series, it is a missing half of
      // the design: with no pre-intervention points there is no trend to
      // extrapolate, so there is no counterfactual and nothing to contrast
      // against. It used to export as "0 pre-intervention points" under a
      // document with no incomplete banner at all.
      const seg = (p, which, why) =>
        p.state === "blank" ? "the number of " + which + "-intervention points"
        : p.state === "bad" ? "the number of " + which + "-intervention points (“" + p.raw + "” is not a whole number of periods)"
        : p.n === 0 ? "the number of " + which + "-intervention points (0 was entered, and " + why + ")"
        : "";
      return [
        [!s.interruptDate, "the interruption date — the design has no pre/post segments without it"],
        [!s.population, "the population"],
        [!s.outcome, "the outcome"],
        [!s.intervention, "the intervention / policy"],
        [!s.data, "the data source"],
        [!!seg(pp, "pre", "a trend has to be estimated before it can be extrapolated"), seg(pp, "pre", "a trend has to be estimated before it can be extrapolated")],
        [!!seg(qp, "post", "there is nothing after the interruption to contrast against the counterfactual"), seg(qp, "post", "there is nothing after the interruption to contrast against the counterfactual")],
        [s.model === "poisson" && !s.denom, "the denominator / offset for the rate model"],
        [s.controlled && !s.controlSeries, "the control series (controlled ITS is ticked but no comparison series is named)"],
      ].filter((x) => x[0]).map((x) => x[1]);
    };
    // Everything computeChecks produces used to die on screen: only the
    // `missing()` gaps travelled. So a protocol with every field filled, no
    // seasonality adjustment on monthly data and no autocorrelation handling —
    // the two things that most often invalidate an ITS — exported as a clean,
    // complete-looking document with no trace of either warning, while the page
    // that built it showed both in amber. The reader of the Word file is the
    // person who most needs them.
    const docChecks = (s) => computeChecks(s, true);
    const blockers = (s) => docChecks(s).filter((c) => c.level === "error").map((c) => c.msg);
    const cautions = (s) => docChecks(s).filter((c) => c.level === "warn").map((c) => c.msg);
    const BLK_HEAD = "This draft is not ready to be used as a protocol. The items below must be settled first; until then the sections that depend on them say what is missing rather than supplying a value.";
    const CAU_HEAD = "Flagged while this protocol was drafted. These are not blocking, but each is a choice a reader will expect to see justified.";

    const buildMarkdown = (s) => {
      const today = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);
      const P = or(s.population, "(population)"), O = or(s.outcome, "(outcome)"), I = or(s.intervention, "(intervention/policy)");
      const D = or(s.interruptDate, "(interruption date)");
      const quote = (head, list) => list.length ? "> **" + head + "**\n" + list.map((m) => ">\n> - " + m).join("\n") + "\n\n" : "";
      return `# ${or(s.title, "(Untitled interrupted time series study)")}

_Study protocol · interrupted time series (segmented regression) · drafted ${today}_

${quote(BLK_HEAD, blockers(s))}${quote(CAU_HEAD, cautions(s))}${window.PC.abstractMd(pcOf(s))}
## 1. Background and rationale
This study uses an **interrupted time series (ITS)** design to evaluate whether **${I}** changed the **level** and/or the **slope (trend)** of **${O}** in **${P}**.

*In plain terms:* ${I} took effect on a known date. We take the way ${O} was already trending before that date, draw that trend forward as what would have happened anyway, and measure how far the observed outcome departs from it.

*Precisely:* the outcome is aggregated into equal ${unitPhrase(s.unit)} intervals and regressed on time, an interruption indicator, and time-since-interruption. Their coefficients separate the **baseline trend**, the **change in level** (the immediate step at the interruption) and the **change in slope** (how the trend bends afterwards). These answer different questions and can point in opposite directions — an intervention can cause a sharp one-off fall that then drifts back up — so both are reported (Wagner 2002; Lopez Bernal 2017).${s.controlled ? " A **control series** that shares the secular environment but is not exposed to the intervention is added, so the estimate becomes the *difference* in level and slope changes and any co-intervention affecting both series cancels (Lopez Bernal 2018)." : " Without a control series, every departure from the extrapolated pre-trend is attributed to the intervention, including anything else that happened at the same time."}

## 2. Question
Among **${P}**, did **${I}** (interrupted at **${D}**) change the level and/or slope of **${O}**?

## 3. Study design
A ${s.controlled ? "**controlled** " : "single-group "}**interrupted time series** analysed by segmented regression, using observational data from **${or(s.data, "(data source)")}**. Time unit: **${s.unit}**. Series length: **${ptsText(prePts(s))}** pre-intervention and **${ptsText(postPts(s))}** post-intervention points.

## 4. Time series construction
The outcome is aggregated per **${unitPhrase(s.unit)}** interval across the study window (${ptsText(prePts(s))} pre + ${ptsText(postPts(s))} post points).${s.model === "poisson" ? ` The denominator / offset is: ${or(s.denom, "(person-time / eligible population; entered as a log offset)")}.` : ""} Each interval is indexed by a continuous **time** variable; an interruption indicator marks intervals from **${D}** onward; and a **time-since-interruption** variable is **coded 0 at the first affected period** and counts up from there. ${CODING_NOTE}${s.lag ? ` A transition / phase-in period is handled as: ${s.lag}.` : ""}

## 5. Intervention and interruption
- **Intervention / policy:** ${I}
- **Interruption date:** ${D}
- **Transition / phase-in:** ${or(s.lag, "none (a single, sharp interruption is assumed)")}

## 6. Model specification
The outcome is modelled with ${modelText(s.model)}. The segmented model estimates: (i) the **baseline level** and **pre-intervention slope**; (ii) the **change in level** (immediate step at the interruption); and (iii) the **change in slope** (post- minus pre-intervention trend). ${seasonText(s.seasonality)}. ${autocorrText(s.autocorr)}.${s.controlled ? ` A control series (${or(s.controlSeries, "a comparison group/region/outcome unaffected by the intervention")}) is included, with group-by-segment interaction terms so the **difference** in level and slope changes (intervention minus control) is the primary estimate.` : ""}

## 7. Estimand and effect measures
The primary estimands are ${effectText(s)}.

We also report the **absolute and relative difference** between the observed outcome and ${cfText(s)}, with 95% confidence intervals, ${HORIZON_AT}. ${HORIZON_WHY}

## 8. Statistical analysis
1. Construct the aggregated time series and the segmented-regression design matrix (time, interruption, time-since-interruption${s.controlled ? ", control-group, and their interactions" : ""}).
2. Fit the ${s.model === "linear" ? "segmented linear" : "segmented Poisson (or negative-binomial)"} model. ${autocorrText(s.autocorr)}.
3. ${s.seasonality === "none" ? "Assess seasonality and, if present, adjust for it" : "Fit the seasonality terms specified in §6 (" + SEASON_LABEL[s.seasonality] + ") and report how many terms were used"}.
4. Report the level change and slope change (with 95% CI), plot the observed series with the fitted segments and the counterfactual, and estimate the difference-from-counterfactual ${HORIZON_AT}.
